This document specifies an in-place comparison sorting algorithm. The design goal is a sorting routine with a predictable worst-case time complexity and with no heap allocations during execution. The algorithm is based on merge sort, following a divide-and-conquer strategy, and it falls back to insertion sort on small subarrays.

The sorter operates on a dynamically sized array of 32-bit signed integers. The comparison function takes two such integers and returns a boolean that indicates whether the first argument is ordered before the second argument.

The top-level sort operation takes a mutable reference to the array and the comparison function as its arguments, and it returns a boolean indicating success. A precondition of calling sort is that the array pointer must not be null. The merge-sort helper performs the recursive split on a subarray bounded by a low index and a high index, recursively sorts the left and right halves, and then invokes merge to combine them. The merge operation takes the array, a low index, a midpoint index, and a high index, assumes that the two halves are already sorted, and merges them in order.

After sort returns, for every adjacent pair of elements in the array, the comparison function must report them in non-decreasing order. The algorithm must run in time bounded by n log n comparisons, and it must use no more than linear additional auxiliary space for the merge buffer.

Case one asserts that sorting an empty array returns true without touching any memory. Case two asserts that sorting a single-element array is a no-op that also returns true. The edge case of interest is sorting an already-reversed input, which exercises the worst path through the recursion.

Recursing on very small subarrays is a known inefficiency: the constant overhead of the recursive call exceeds the cost of simply insertion-sorting the small fragment. The preferred approach is to fall back to insertion sort when a subarray becomes shorter than sixteen elements.
